{
  "title": "match statements",
  "summary": "Structural pattern matching: switch, and much more.",
  "minutes": 10,
  "steps": [
    {
      "type": "learn",
      "title": "match / case",
      "body": [
        "Python 3.10 added `match`, which looks like C#'s `switch`:",
        "",
        "```python",
        "match command:",
        "    case \"start\":",
        "        print(\"starting\")",
        "    case \"stop\" | \"halt\":        # either value",
        "        print(\"stopping\")",
        "    case _:                      # default",
        "        print(\"unknown\")",
        "```",
        "",
        "No `break` needed and no fall-through. `_` matches anything."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "predict",
      "prompt": "What does this print?",
      "code": [
        "for code in [200, 404, 418, 500]:",
        "    match code:",
        "        case 200:",
        "            print(\"ok\")",
        "        case 404 | 410:",
        "            print(\"gone\")",
        "        case 500:",
        "            print(\"error\")",
        "        case _:",
        "            print(\"other\")"
      ],
      "answer": [
        "ok",
        "gone",
        "other",
        "error"
      ],
      "explain": "Each value is tested against the cases in order; `_` catches 418."
    },
    {
      "type": "learn",
      "title": "Patterns capture",
      "body": [
        "`match` really shines on **structure**. A pattern can take a sequence apart and bind names, like C# list patterns:",
        "",
        "```python",
        "match line.split():",
        "    case [\"go\", direction]:",
        "        print(\"moving\", direction)",
        "    case [\"take\", item, *rest]:",
        "        print(\"taking\", item)",
        "    case [] :",
        "        print(\"say something\")",
        "    case _:",
        "        print(\"pardon?\")",
        "```",
        "",
        "`direction` is **bound** to whatever is in that position. `*rest` collects the remainder. A `case` can add a guard: `case [n] if n.isdigit():`."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "mcq",
      "prompt": "Which input reaches `case [\"take\", item, *rest]`?",
      "options": [
        "`take lamp and key`",
        "`take`",
        "`grab lamp`",
        "an empty line"
      ],
      "answer": 0,
      "explain": "The list must start with `take` and have at least one more word; `*rest` may be empty or hold the remainder."
    },
    {
      "type": "predict",
      "prompt": "What does this print?",
      "code": [
        "for words in [[\"go\", \"north\"], [\"go\"], [\"look\"], [\"drop\", \"key\", \"now\"]]:",
        "    match words:",
        "        case [\"go\", where]:",
        "            print(\"walk\", where)",
        "        case [\"drop\", *things]:",
        "            print(\"drop\", len(things))",
        "        case [single]:",
        "            print(\"verb\", single)"
      ],
      "answer": [
        "walk north",
        "verb go",
        "verb look",
        "drop 2"
      ],
      "explain": "`[\"go\"]` has one element, so it matches `[single]`. `*things` gathers `key` and `now`."
    },
    {
      "type": "blanks",
      "prompt": "Complete the command handler.",
      "code": [
        "match input().split():",
        "    case [__1__, item]:",
        "        print(\"you take\", item)",
        "    case [\"quit\"] __2__ [\"exit\"]:",
        "        print(\"bye\")",
        "    case __3__:",
        "        print(\"?\")"
      ],
      "blanks": [
        "\"take\"",
        "|",
        "_"
      ],
      "bank": [
        "or",
        "default",
        "else",
        "item"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "match",
      "prompt": "Match each pattern to an input it matches.",
      "pairs": [
        [
          "`case [x]:`",
          "`[\"hi\"]`"
        ],
        [
          "`case [x, y]:`",
          "`[1, 2]`"
        ],
        [
          "`case [first, *rest]:`",
          "`[1, 2, 3, 4]`"
        ],
        [
          "`case []:`",
          "`[]`"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "title": "Calculator",
      "prompt": [
        "Read lines like `add 2 3`, `mul 4 5`, `neg 7` until `quit`. Use `match` on the split words:",
        "",
        "- `add A B` prints the sum, `mul A B` the product, `neg A` minus A.",
        "- anything else prints `unknown`.",
        "",
        "On `quit` print `bye` and stop."
      ],
      "starter": "\n",
      "solution": [
        "while True:",
        "    match input().split():",
        "        case [\"add\", a, b]:",
        "            print(int(a) + int(b))",
        "        case [\"mul\", a, b]:",
        "            print(int(a) * int(b))",
        "        case [\"neg\", a]:",
        "            print(-int(a))",
        "        case [\"quit\"]:",
        "            print(\"bye\")",
        "            break",
        "        case _:",
        "            print(\"unknown\")"
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "name": "mixed commands",
          "stdin": "add 2 3\nmul 4 5\nneg 7\nquit\n",
          "expect": [
            "5",
            "20",
            "-7",
            "bye"
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "unknown commands",
          "stdin": "add 1\nhello\n\nquit\n",
          "expect": [
            "unknown",
            "unknown",
            "unknown",
            "bye"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "require": [
        {
          "pattern": "\\bmatch\\b",
          "message": "Uses a match statement"
        }
      ],
      "hints": [
        "`case [\"add\", a, b]:` binds the two numbers as strings.",
        "`break` inside a `case` leaves the enclosing `while` loop."
      ]
    }
  ]
}
